I could not refute this one. The park is real. It is an owner gate by design, but nobody is told about it, and even the owner's normal fixes do not release it. No record is in this state now.

How the record gets here. src/repair/loop-publication.ts:346-357 runs while a `pull_request` intent is still saved. It looks up the PR on the record's own Sentinel branch with findPullRequestByHeadRef (src/github/port-base.ts:117). If that PR has the expected head but its baseRef is not config.baseBranch, it calls markTerminalBlocked (src/repair/transitions.ts:641-657). That sets nextStep=blocked, blocker other/"PR base identity mismatch", intent=null, wait=null and target.pr=null. config.baseBranch is the target repo's default branch, read on every run (src/host/targets.ts:176). Sentinel always opens its PRs against that branch (loop-publication-state.ts:122-129), so getting here needs a person or a config change while a `pull_request` intent is still saved:
- the owner retargets the PR's base after a create whose result was ambiguous, failed, or crossed the run deadline;
- the repo's default branch changes in that same window;
- a person opens a PR from the Sentinel branch to another base after a deadline-crossed push. The port then refuses because the author is not trusted (port-publication.ts:150-165), loop-publication-state.ts:118-131 keeps the intent plus a 5-minute wait, and the next run's reconcile writes this park.
These are real GitHub inputs, so the state is reachable, but only through rare human action.

Nothing moves it while the issue is open:
- **Runtime:** selection.ts:242-244 skips blocked records. reconcileOrphanPublication needs nextStep=work (loop-publication.ts:470). The only blocked-record recoveries in the runtime match other messages (loop-review-recovery task acceptance; loop-legacy-loss missing_evidence; review-round-cap CI_RED prefixes). Intake will not re-admit the issue because its record id already exists (loop-intake.ts:311-318).
- **Launcher (3f041b46):** I went through every planner that reads blocker.message. None matches this message:
  - retry table (hosted-autonomy-policy.ts:252-370);
  - retries.ts:589: with no matching rule it only tries planCiFirstReadmission, then silently continues;
  - ci-first, ci-retries, ci-rerun;
  - conflict and base-refresh retries;
  - refresh-rearm (REFUSED_REFRESH);
  - candidate-refs (only "candidate branch ref identity mismatch");
  - mismatch, preservation and historical dispositions;
  - issue48;
  - spent-delivery, which also needs pr!==null;
  - closures.ts:155-160, which keeps an already blocked record with no PR on its own blocker even after the issue closes.
- **Checker:** I re-ran the census for this class in wt81-verify. Head CI green and head CI red each gave absorbing=true, visible=false, movedBy=null, 16 real runHostedAutonomy passes, and no action line naming the record.

Nothing tells the owner. The only output is the runtime's idle line "N records: x terminal, y blocked, z waiting" (loop-cycle.ts:370-383), a bare count. sentinel-cli status lists workflow runs and merges only. local-status.yml covers local runs only. The reason exists only in the state ref's JSON.

The owner's natural fixes do not work either:
- Retargeting the PR back leaves the record blocked, because nothing re-reads the PR.
- Closing the PR unmerged does not retire it (target.pr is null), which breaks the "PR closed unmerged means retired" rule.
- Merging into the other base does not close the issue.
Only a hand edit of the state releases it. The sibling parks "PR head identity mismatch" (loop-publication.ts:372) and "PR publication identity mismatch" (loop-publication-state.ts:144) have the same shape.

Live check (read-only):
- No work record on origin/sentinel-state/repair carries this message.
- `git log -S` over the available history, back to 2026-09-25, never shows it written. As a control, "candidate branch ref identity mismatch" is found in 4 commits.
- Both targets' default branch is development. All open PRs on Sentinel branches target development: 1 on ubiquity/sentinel and 48 on ai.ubq.fi.

Proposed fix. The owner line alone is safe (it writes nothing) and turns this into a visible owner gate. But it should name a remedy that actually works, and today none does short of editing the state, so it is not enough on its own.

