Two parts. Neither refunds anything, closes PRs or deletes branches.

(1) Smallest safe fix now, launcher only, no runtime change: the proposed per-pass owner line, placed where the existing visible owner gates already speak. In ops/hosted-autonomy-retries.ts, in the `if (rule === undefined)` branch at line 589, before planCiFirstReadmission: when the blocker is kind "other" and exactly one of "PR head identity mismatch", "PR base identity mismatch" or "PR publication identity mismatch", call onDenied({id, repository, reason: "owner_gate:pr_identity_mismatch"}) and continue. The retry pass then prints retry:<id>:denied:owner_gate:pr_identity_mismatch on every pass (ops/hosted-autonomy-retry-pass.ts:396/475), the same mechanism that makes conflict_resolution_protected visible. The closed-issue skip at the top of the loop keeps it quiet once the issue closes. In the checker, these classes move from KNOWN_JAMS to visible owner parks.

(2) The real fix in the next runtime generation: stop false-positive parking on stale closed PRs, and never open a replacement PR, in line with the user's "resume off existing PRs". In src/repair/loop-publication.ts:334-376, judge the intent against OPEN PRs only, as reconcileOrphanPublication and port-publication.ts already do:
- one open PR at the expected head and base: adopt it (add state === "open" at line 345, so a closed PR is never adopted);
- one open PR with a different head or base: keep the owner gate, which is now visible through (1);
- no open PR, but a closed own PR (trusted author, exact headRef) that is unmerged: retire it under the owner rule for a PR closed unmerged, which is terminal by design. Keep the PR number as evidence; do not call createPullRequestFor. If it is merged, use RETIRED_MERGED_MESSAGE;
- no PR at all: createPullRequestFor as today.

Also make the reconcile list all PRs and filter to open ones, so two or more closed PRs no longer end in the "unavailable" re-poll loop. Pair this with the KNOWN_JAMS J2 pr_closed_by_owner fix (retire on owner close instead of republishing) to remove the closed-PR precondition entirely.

Re-admit the record only when the launcher positively reads the issue as open, with the exact issue number, rather than on "not known closed".

1. In readHostedClosedIssues (ops/hosted-autonomy-retry-pass.ts:160-169), also collect an `openIssues` set: add the hostedIssueKey only when the read returns true. Make readIssueOpen check that the returned `number` matches for every repository, not only Gateway VPS, so a transferred issue does not read as open.
2. Pass `openIssues` to planHostedRetries and add a dedicated rule next to planCiFirstReadmission. It applies when:
   - nextStep is "blocked", the blocker kind is "stale_source" and the message is exactly "source issue is not open: no task-bound review can be requested";
   - openIssues has the record's issue key;
   - intentClosable holds;
   - head, pr and branch are all non-null;
   - reviewRoundsLeft(snapshot, planned, cutoff) > 0, where planned uses plannedReviewRounds(record, settledReviewAttempts(...));
   - legacyFinalRoundAdmissible holds.
   It returns nextStep "review" with those reviewRounds, grant 0, no base advance and detail `stale_source:<message>`. This is the same identity space as the existing review-returning rows.
3. The retry pass already checks that the PR is open before admitting a plan. The runtime then re-runs freshness, CI first, the round ceiling and the live issue read inside requestReviewFor, so the 3-round cap, CI first, charged reservations and never closing PRs all still hold.
4. A record whose issue reads closed, missing or unreadable stays parked with no write. Optionally emit `retry:<id>:denied:source_not_open` so the park is visible.
5. Remove "J1:park:rev.stale_source@new" from KNOWN_JAMS in tests/model/jams.ts. Add census worlds where the issue read returns null, 404 or a different number, to prove there is no loop of re-admitting and re-parking.

For a round-0 head this route reaches requestReviewFor via an unbound observation, a review_quota wait and the expiry recovery, so it waits at most REVIEW_BOUND_MS (20 minutes). The rev.task_shape@new park has the same shape (only a human edit to the issue clears it) and can use the same positive-read rule, keyed on readIssueTask returning a bounded statement.

The proposed fix is sound but has to be narrow: in the retry pass, only for this exact blocker with `pr === null`, restore the PR the way `judge()` in ops/hosted-autonomy-refresh-rearm.ts does. Required checks:
- the record has `candidateBranch(record.id)`;
- the branch tip and the preservation ref are both at `target.head`;
- exactly one open, unmerged PR from that branch, by a trusted author, against the configured base, with `headSha === head`.

Then issue the review grant with `reviewRounds = plannedReviewRounds(...)`, so the next charge is a fresh identity. Nothing is refunded and the settled charge still counts toward the hard 3-round cap through `reviewRoundsLeft`. If `reviewRoundsLeft` is 0, refuse with an `onDenied` line so it shows as an owner-visible park. Any failed check should also log a line (for example `retry:<id>:denied:review_publication_unverified`) instead of the silent `continue`. That one-line `onDenied` alone is the smallest safe launcher change: it turns this silent J1 into a visible owner park.

Better, at the root (runtime, next generation): at src/repair/loop-review-request.ts:232-246, stop parking on a settled duplicate. Either raise `counters.reviewRounds` past the settled identity and retry under `reviewRoundCeilingGate`, which is the launcher's own `plannedReviewRounds` rule, or at least use `markBlocked` instead of `markTerminalBlocked` so `target.pr` is kept. The PR is still open; only the review identity is dead. With the PR kept, the existing rule already moves the record, as the probe showed.

The proposed per-pass owner line alone would make this a visible owner gate, but a PR that should merge under the owner rules would still wait for a human. Smaller and better, all in ops/ (no runtime release needed):

(1) Add a launcher re-admission for a blocked record that has all of:
- kind "other" and a message starting with REVIEW_ROUND_CEILING_CARRIED_CI_PENDING_PREFIX;
- an open issue;
- an open PR whose live head equals record.target.head;
- every latest check on that head completed and green (the retry pass already reads this into ciGreenHeads);
- blocker.since earlier than that CI's latest completedAt, so it re-admits at most once per head unless someone re-runs CI.

The rule returns the record to `work` with the blocker and wait cleared. It charges and refunds nothing. Gate it on the R80+ carried-runtime allowlist, because R79 would review a refreshed head at `work` past the cap (loop-carried-delivery.ts:39-43).

The runtime then re-derives the patch proof, the tip (any further refresh stays under CARRIED_REFRESH_LIMIT), green CI and task acceptance, and writes the carried-merge marker. The launcher merges at exactly the tip, so a red head never merges and no review round is spent.

Re-admit within 6h of the green completion; otherwise the runtime re-parks it as CARRIED_STALLED. Apply the same rule to REVIEWED_CI_PENDING, returning it to `delivery`.

(2) For parks that stay (head still pending, no check runs, or red with no base evidence), emit `owner-gate:<id>:carried_ci_pending:<labels>` on every pass so they become visible owner gates. Optionally give the red-without-base-evidence branch (loop-carried-delivery.ts:557-567) its own prefix, since that CI did finish.

A smaller defensive fix in the launcher only, covering REVIEWED and not BASE_MOVED, built on the existing rule at ops/hosted-autonomy-retries.ts:648.

1. In planHostedRetries, before the `rule === undefined` fallback (:589), plan {nextStep: "delivery"} for a blocked record only when all of these hold:
   - kind is "other" and the message is exactly REVIEW_ROUND_CEILING_REVIEWED;
   - intent is null, and the issue is open (the closedIssues gate);
   - ordinaryReviewRoundsSpent, and the runtime is proven CI-first (ciFirst);
   - the base tip is known and differs from target.base;
   - the head's key is in ciGreenHeads;
   - boundReviewReceipts is non-empty (a clean App review of the exact head and base).
   Leave target.pr as it is. Do not count the plan as a CI correction (use the CI_GREEN_DETAIL_SUFFIX so ciCorrectionPlan excludes it).
2. Let REVIEWED parks into the spentReads filter at ops/hosted-autonomy-retry-pass.ts:336-341, which is bounded and rotating, so their head CI gets read.

Ranking: spentOnMovedBase never writes REVIEWED. A re-admitted record therefore either takes the carried refresh, which is bounded by CARRIED_REFRESH_LIMIT=6 and the 6-hour wait bounds, and merges through carriedMergeGate, or it lands in a different park class (CI checks failed, reviewed-CI-pending, BASE_MOVED, or the carried parks) that this rule never re-admits. So the rule fires at most once per park: no J2 loop, nothing charged, a red head never merges.

Longer term, in the runtime (next generation):
- At loop-work.ts:176-178, when the latest clean, accepted review binds the exact head and base (headAcceptedByReview), use advanceToDelivery instead of the REVIEWED park. The ordinary delivery path then merges at the exact base, or does the carried refresh on a moved base, so the REVIEWED dead end can no longer be reached.
- Make ceilingParkMessage check the base. A REVIEWED park whose review is on a different base than target.base is skipped by the delivery pass without any log line (boundReviewReceipts is empty and carriedMergeReceipt needs `work`, ops/hosted-autonomy-delivery-pass.ts:204). That variant should park as BASE_MOVED, or go to delivery, instead.

Smallest safe fix, launcher only, works with R80 as installed. In the delivery pass, take a park that is BASE_MOVED (or REVIEWED with a moved tip) where all of these hold:
- `preMergeGate` refused with `base_moved` only because the moved-base merge is off;
- `latestAuthorizingReceipt` just returned "authorized" under the live task;
- `headIsGreen` holds on the exact head;
- the issue is open and the pull request is open at that head;
- there is no intent.

Hand that record back to the runtime with the runtime's own transition, `spentDelivered`/`advanceToDelivery` (blocker and wait cleared; R79 parses this shape), and log `delivery:<id>:carried_readmitted`. Make the rule inert unless the authenticated installed runtime is an allowlisted revision that carries the refresh (R80 e48e7e110 and later), as the other re-admission rules do. Limit it to once per 24h per record, measured from the park's updatedAt, so a runtime re-park can't cycle unbounded. At `delivery`, the runtime's spentOnMovedBase sees a green head with an authorizing review, then deliveryQueueDeferral, the one refresh intent and the carried merge. That path is already bounded by CARRIED_REFRESH_LIMIT and CARRIED_WAIT_BOUND_MS. It charges nothing and refunds nothing, spends no review round, and the carried gate still requires the refreshed head's own CI to be green, so a red head never merges. When the latest review does not authorize, the launcher should not keep the park as "deliverable". It should emit an explicit owner line on every pass, e.g. `owner:<id>:task_changed_after_final_review`, which makes it a visible owner gate. Follow-up for the next runtime: in spentOnMovedBase, write precise parks instead of BASE_MOVED. A task-digest refusal on a clean, exact receipt should wait (bounded) on the task context and then park "the issue text changed after the last review". No exact-head review should park UNREVIEWED, and P0/P1 should park REJECTED. BASE_MOVED is then never written for an authorizing review and can come off REVIEW_ROUND_CEILING_DELIVERABLE_MESSAGES. Also add the "task text changed at delivery.gate" edge to tests/model/pipeline.ts so the checker reaches this positive from the lifecycle instead of only from the census.
